@page "/"

<PageTitle>Weather Alert ML Demo</PageTitle>

<div class="container">
    <div class="hero">
        <h1>🌩️ Weather Alert ML Demo</h1>
        <p>
            A demonstration of machine learning in .NET using ML.NET.
            Two models — <strong>Decision Tree</strong> and <strong>Random Forest</strong> —
            are trained on real historical weather data from Open-Meteo and used
            to predict rainfall, humidity and tornado alerts for subscribers.
        </p>

        <div class="hero-actions">
            <a href="/subscribe" class="btn-primary">Subscribe to Alerts</a>
            <a href="/dashboard" class="btn-secondary">View Dashboard</a>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
            <h3>🧠 How it works</h3>
            <p>
                90 days of historical weather data is fetched from Open-Meteo
                and used to train two ML models. Each row is automatically
                labelled as alert or normal based on weather thresholds.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
            <h3>🌲 Decision Tree</h3>
            <p>
                Uses gradient boosted decision trees (FastTree) to learn
                patterns in weather conditions and predict alert probability
                with a confidence score.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
            <h3>🌳 Random Forest</h3>
            <p>
                Builds an ensemble of independent trees (FastForest), each
                trained on a random feature subset. More stable than a single
                tree — final prediction is a vote across all trees.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
            <h3>📧 Alerts</h3>
            <p>
                When alert conditions are predicted, an email is sent to the
                subscriber showing both model results side by side so you can
                compare Decision Tree vs Random Forest predictions.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>